WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.217 --> 00:00:05.069
[MUSIC]

00:00:05.069 --> 00:00:09.521
&gt;&gt; It was past midnight, but I was ready.

00:00:09.521 --> 00:00:11.911
The next day, I meet with the CEO and

00:00:11.911 --> 00:00:15.427
other senior leaders of my
global consulting firm.

00:00:15.427 --> 00:00:20.117
I talked about the work we were doing as
employee resource groups and our progress,

00:00:20.117 --> 00:00:21.994
and diversity, and inclusion.

00:00:21.994 --> 00:00:25.523
For the past two years,
I had been leading our LGBTQ and

00:00:25.523 --> 00:00:29.535
ally employee resource group and
we were doing great things.

00:00:29.535 --> 00:00:33.357
We had expanded to over
25 offices globally.

00:00:33.357 --> 00:00:38.691
We were increasing awareness and
support across geographies.

00:00:38.691 --> 00:00:42.226
These are just a few of the wins
that I was really excited to

00:00:42.226 --> 00:00:43.783
talk about the next day.

00:00:43.783 --> 00:00:48.487
Earlier, I had rehearsed with the head
of our diversity and inclusion team.

00:00:48.487 --> 00:00:51.295
She was thrilled by the presentation.

00:00:51.295 --> 00:00:54.693
There was just one last
thing that I had to do.

00:00:54.693 --> 00:00:58.331
She asked me if I was going to wear
my nail polish to the meeting.

00:00:58.331 --> 00:01:00.987
Now, I didn't think much
of it in the moment.

00:01:00.987 --> 00:01:05.097
I would often take off my nail
polish to meet with clients.

00:01:05.097 --> 00:01:08.009
But that night, it felt different.

00:01:08.009 --> 00:01:10.054
I couldn't help, but

00:01:10.054 --> 00:01:16.091
feel as I went finger by finger that
I was removing a part of myself.

00:01:16.091 --> 00:01:19.841
Now, I get that nail polish
seems like something trivial.

00:01:19.841 --> 00:01:24.289
But to me, it is so tied to my identity.

00:01:24.289 --> 00:01:28.827
It's like my cross or
my hijab in that I wear it as

00:01:28.827 --> 00:01:32.571
a symbol to world about my queerness and

00:01:32.571 --> 00:01:38.375
I wear it as a symbol to myself
about who I am, and my journey.

00:01:38.375 --> 00:01:42.796
I wear it like armor to feel confident and

00:01:42.796 --> 00:01:49.569
to overcome the insecurity that
I have felt for so many years.

00:01:49.569 --> 00:01:52.356
So that night, I had an uh-huh moment.

00:01:52.356 --> 00:01:58.278
I thought maybe I've slipped
through a crack in our DNI.

00:01:58.278 --> 00:02:03.347
Tonight, I'm going to talk about the work
being done in diversity and inclusion.

00:02:03.347 --> 00:02:07.376
I'm going to talk about what's going
well and how it can be improved.

00:02:07.376 --> 00:02:12.585
In doing so, I'm going to emphasize
the importance of thinking

00:02:12.585 --> 00:02:17.894
more about individuality in our
work in DNI as practitioners,

00:02:17.894 --> 00:02:20.513
business leaders and people.

00:02:20.513 --> 00:02:25.519
So for those of you who aren't quite sure
what DNI is, diversity and inclusion.

00:02:25.519 --> 00:02:29.881
It's a firm's formal set of activities
to create a workplace where

00:02:29.881 --> 00:02:33.336
people of all backgrounds
feel welcomed and valued.

00:02:33.336 --> 00:02:37.137
It includes things like non-bias
trainings, mentorship programs and

00:02:37.137 --> 00:02:38.638
recruitment initiatives.

00:02:38.638 --> 00:02:42.852
When it's done well,
it can have business impacts.

00:02:42.852 --> 00:02:47.771
In fact, in a recent survey,
85% of CEOs said that DNI

00:02:47.771 --> 00:02:51.773
programs were improving
their bottom lines.

00:02:51.773 --> 00:02:55.317
They talked about things
like greater innovation,

00:02:55.317 --> 00:02:58.870
access to new markets and
retaining talent better.

00:02:58.870 --> 00:03:04.506
So it's clear that DNI can
improve business performance,

00:03:04.506 --> 00:03:08.233
but DNI is not just about bottom lines.

00:03:08.233 --> 00:03:13.095
When DNI has done well,
it can have big impacts on individuals.

00:03:13.095 --> 00:03:15.623
That means all of us.

00:03:15.623 --> 00:03:19.086
Even with my story about nail polish,

00:03:19.086 --> 00:03:23.536
I would still argue that
DNI is still essential.

00:03:23.536 --> 00:03:30.682
As a queer person, I have a long
complicated relationship with my identity.

00:03:30.682 --> 00:03:34.443
So when I'm in a workplace
where I feel included,

00:03:34.443 --> 00:03:39.295
I don't have to stress about fitting it or
if I'm being judged.

00:03:39.295 --> 00:03:42.871
I can put all of my brain
power into my work.

00:03:42.871 --> 00:03:48.251
I deliver better results and
I feel motivated to do so.

00:03:48.251 --> 00:03:52.684
Now I assume others in
the audience tonight have had

00:03:52.684 --> 00:03:57.427
similar experiences about DNI and
feeling included,

00:03:57.427 --> 00:04:01.873
and that underscores the value of DNI for
all of us.

00:04:01.873 --> 00:04:06.554
So now that we've talked a little
bit about why DNI is important,

00:04:06.554 --> 00:04:11.065
I wanted to think about the ways
that are current approaches may

00:04:11.065 --> 00:04:13.210
actually be holding us back.

00:04:13.210 --> 00:04:17.434
But traditionally, DNI is organized
around categorical groups.

00:04:17.434 --> 00:04:21.440
Think women, veterans, people of color.

00:04:21.440 --> 00:04:27.996
This is great and it does bring a lot of
value to firms, but it's not perfect.

00:04:27.996 --> 00:04:30.905
I'll focus on two
limitations in particular.

00:04:30.905 --> 00:04:35.821
The first is how we think about
the diversity within categories and

00:04:35.821 --> 00:04:39.420
the second is how we
handle intersectionality,

00:04:39.420 --> 00:04:43.743
which is the interplay across
our different identities.

00:04:43.743 --> 00:04:48.017
So for this first idea of
the diversity within categories,

00:04:48.017 --> 00:04:50.673
let's go back to my original story.

00:04:50.673 --> 00:04:56.508
It's true that we were having a lot
of success for the LGBTQ community.

00:04:56.508 --> 00:05:03.680
But as I realized firsthand, it wasn't
success for everyone in that community.

00:05:03.680 --> 00:05:09.887
More specifically, we weren't doing enough
for trans and queer people like myself.

00:05:09.887 --> 00:05:13.255
See, there was a problem
with working with the group.

00:05:13.255 --> 00:05:16.743
We weren't seeing who
wasn't at the table and

00:05:16.743 --> 00:05:20.415
we were getting a false
positive on our success.

00:05:20.415 --> 00:05:25.341
So as companies work on DNI,
they need to look critically at

00:05:25.341 --> 00:05:29.389
the composition of their
diverse communities.

00:05:29.389 --> 00:05:38.078
Ask in my company, does LGBTQ
actually include L, G, B, T and Q?

00:05:38.078 --> 00:05:44.178
In any company, is every woman white or
is every black employee a man?

00:05:44.178 --> 00:05:49.706
We need to take these into
consideration to advance our DNI.

00:05:49.706 --> 00:05:53.624
So having talked about this first
limitation around the diversity within

00:05:53.624 --> 00:05:58.000
categories, let's think about the second
limitation around intersexuality.

00:05:58.000 --> 00:06:02.635
Again, this is the interplay
across our different identities.

00:06:02.635 --> 00:06:06.485
And for this, I'm going to talk about
the experience of my friend Erica,

00:06:06.485 --> 00:06:07.330
a black woman.

00:06:07.330 --> 00:06:13.077
Again, traditional DNI would organize
programs around categorical groups and

00:06:13.077 --> 00:06:16.458
there would be programs for
black employees and

00:06:16.458 --> 00:06:19.851
others for women and
Erica would opt in to both.

00:06:19.851 --> 00:06:25.561
But in her words, she wouldn't
really be there as her full self.

00:06:25.561 --> 00:06:28.063
She'd be black Erica had a lunch.

00:06:28.063 --> 00:06:31.297
And later that day,
woman Erica had a dinner.

00:06:31.297 --> 00:06:35.085
It sounds strange, right?

00:06:35.085 --> 00:06:40.663
Now we're all different, but we're all
similar in that we are all complex.

00:06:40.663 --> 00:06:43.968
We're all a combination of identities.

00:06:43.968 --> 00:06:47.733
Some visible, most invisible.

00:06:47.733 --> 00:06:52.186
And so we can all have this experience
where it's hard to be present

00:06:52.186 --> 00:06:56.495
as our full selves when we're
only being viewed as categories.

00:06:56.495 --> 00:07:01.289
So when working in DNI, we need to make
sure we're designing it for whole people.

00:07:04.479 --> 00:07:07.291
So having talked about these
two limitations, the first,

00:07:07.291 --> 00:07:08.986
the diversity within categories.

00:07:08.986 --> 00:07:12.318
And the second, intersectionality.

00:07:12.318 --> 00:07:15.315
But think of what we can
do to get beyond them.

00:07:15.315 --> 00:07:20.862
I would suggest that working more around
this idea of individuality is a start and

00:07:20.862 --> 00:07:22.749
what might that look like?

00:07:22.749 --> 00:07:25.818
Well, it could take
the shape of storytelling.

00:07:25.818 --> 00:07:29.580
Think of the impact of Erica's and
my stories tonight.

00:07:29.580 --> 00:07:34.423
Think of the impact they and others
could have in sparking discussions, and

00:07:34.423 --> 00:07:36.322
shaping workplace culture.

00:07:36.322 --> 00:07:42.124
It could take the shape of events
that celebrate intersectionality and

00:07:42.124 --> 00:07:46.097
all of our identities,
visible and invisible.

00:07:46.097 --> 00:07:47.195
Taken together,

00:07:47.195 --> 00:07:51.957
these kinds of programs help us overcome
the current limitation of our work.

00:07:51.957 --> 00:07:56.133
We can see and
support new pools of talent.

00:07:56.133 --> 00:07:58.343
We can let people show up and

00:07:58.343 --> 00:08:03.450
participate as their whole selves,
and not just their parts.

00:08:03.450 --> 00:08:08.866
Each of these incremental
gains improves inclusively,

00:08:08.866 --> 00:08:17.120
which would attract new forms of diversity
which should lead to even better outcomes.

00:08:17.120 --> 00:08:19.703
When I reflect on my experience,

00:08:19.703 --> 00:08:24.697
I probably wouldn’t have joined
my firm if it didn’t have DNI.

00:08:24.697 --> 00:08:29.666
I probably would have opted out of
consulting and business altogether.

00:08:29.666 --> 00:08:35.134
For me, it would have been
easier not to exist in those

00:08:35.134 --> 00:08:41.463
worlds than to feel unwelcome or
excluded, but what if I did?

00:08:41.463 --> 00:08:48.547
What value would have been lost to
my company, my clients and myself?

00:08:48.547 --> 00:08:52.367
DNI gave me a reason to opt-in.

00:08:52.367 --> 00:08:59.268
And while it's not perfect, it certainly
part of the reason why I'm here now.

00:08:59.268 --> 00:09:02.282
So in preparing for this moment,

00:09:02.282 --> 00:09:07.135
I had to ask myself,
should I take off my nail polish?

00:09:07.135 --> 00:09:11.622
I can still be insecure
in a lot of settings,

00:09:11.622 --> 00:09:18.672
especially ones like this, but
I'm proud to stand up here tonight.

00:09:18.672 --> 00:09:22.632
Armor on and ready to go to battle for

00:09:22.632 --> 00:09:28.440
a greater focus on individuality
in our work in DNI,

00:09:28.440 --> 00:09:34.386
because diversity and
inclusion is business smart.

00:09:34.386 --> 00:09:38.729
But designing it for individuals,

00:09:38.729 --> 00:09:42.355
that's what makes it human.

00:09:42.355 --> 00:09:43.000
Thank you.

00:09:43.000 --> 00:09:44.654
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:09:44.654 --> 00:09:49.438
[MUSIC]

